# Commit message guidance

Copy this into the contributing guide and adjust the types and scopes.

## Format

```text
<type>[optional scope][!]: <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

- Types: `feat` (new behavior), `fix` (bug fix), `docs`, `refactor`, `perf`,
  `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `style`, `revert`.
- Scope names the area, for example `fix(orders): reject negative quantities`.
- Mark a breaking change with `!` after the type or scope, or with a
  `BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and how to migrate>` footer.
- Write the description in the imperative and describe the effect, not the
  activity (`reject empty input`, not `fixed stuff`).
- Keep trailers in Git trailer format (`Refs: #123`, `Co-authored-by: ...`).

## Checking locally

```text
cz check --commit-msg-file <path to message file>   # what the commit-msg hook runs
cz check --rev-range origin/main..HEAD              # validate a whole branch
```

The hook runs at the `commit-msg` stage; it exists only after hooks are
installed. If it rejects a message, rewrite the message. Do not use
`git commit --no-verify`.

## CI

CI validates the commit range, or the pull-request title when the repository
uses squash merges, because hooks can be skipped or never installed. A failing
title or range check blocks the merge.

## Reviewer checklist

- Does the type match the change (a `feat` adds behavior; a bug fix is `fix`)?
- Is a breaking change marked, with migration guidance in the footer?
- Does the description say what changed for the user or caller?
